.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt EVP_PKEY-SM2 7ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm EVP_PKEY-SM2 ,
.Nm EVP_KEYMGMT-SM2 ,
.Nm SM2
.Nd EVP_PKEY keytype support for the Chinese SM2 signature and encryption algorithms
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Sy SM2
algorithm was first defined by the Chinese national standard GM/T
0003-2012 and was later standardized by ISO as ISO/IEC 14888.
.Sy SM2
is actually an elliptic curve based algorithm.
The current implementation in OpenSSL supports both signature and
encryption schemes via the EVP interface.
.Pp
When doing the
.Sy SM2
signature algorithm, it requires a distinguishing identifier to form the
message prefix which is hashed before the real message is hashed.
.Ss Common SM2 parameters
SM2 uses the parameters defined in
.Xr EVP_PKEY-EC 7 .
The following parameters are different:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqcofactor" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_EC_COFACTOR Ns ) <unsigned integer>
This parameter is ignored for
.Sy SM2 .
.It ( Ns Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_DEFAULT_DIGEST Ns ) <UTF8 string>
Getter that returns the default digest name.
(Currently returns "SM3" as of OpenSSL 3.0).
.El
.Sh NOTES
.Sy SM2
signatures can be generated by using the 'DigestSign' series of APIs,
for instance,
.Xr EVP_DigestSignInit 3 ,
.Xr EVP_DigestSignUpdate 3
and
.Xr EVP_DigestSignFinal 3 .
Ditto for the verification process by calling the 'DigestVerify' series
of APIs.
Note that the SM2 algorithm requires the presence of the public key for
signatures, as such the
.Sy OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_PUB_KEY
option must be set on any key used in signature generation.
.Pp
Before computing an
.Sy SM2
signature, an
.Sy EVP_PKEY_CTX
needs to be created, and an
.Sy SM2
ID must be set for it, like this:
.Bd -literal
 EVP_PKEY_CTX_set1_id(pctx, id, id_len);
.Ed
.Pp
Before calling the
.Xr EVP_DigestSignInit 3
or
.Xr EVP_DigestVerifyInit 3
functions, that
.Sy EVP_PKEY_CTX
should be assigned to the
.Sy EVP_MD_CTX ,
like this:
.Bd -literal
 EVP_MD_CTX_set_pkey_ctx(mctx, pctx);
.Ed
.Pp
There is normally no need to pass a
.Sy pctx
parameter to
.Xr EVP_DigestSignInit 3
or
.Xr EVP_DigestVerifyInit 3
in such a scenario.
.Pp
SM2 can be tested with the
.Xr openssl-speed 1
application since version 3.0. Currently, the only valid algorithm name
is
.Sy sm2 .
.Pp
Since version 3.0, SM2 keys can be generated and loaded only when the
domain parameters specify the SM2 elliptic curve.
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example demonstrates the calling sequence for using an
.Sy EVP_PKEY
to verify a message with the SM2 signature algorithm and the SM3 hash
algorithm:
.Bd -literal
 #include <openssl/evp.h>

 /* obtain an EVP_PKEY using whatever methods... */
 mctx = EVP_MD_CTX_new();
 pctx = EVP_PKEY_CTX_new(pkey, NULL);
 EVP_PKEY_CTX_set1_id(pctx, id, id_len);
 EVP_MD_CTX_set_pkey_ctx(mctx, pctx);
 EVP_DigestVerifyInit(mctx, NULL, EVP_sm3(), NULL, pkey);
 EVP_DigestVerifyUpdate(mctx, msg, msg_len);
 EVP_DigestVerifyFinal(mctx, sig, sig_len)
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_new 3 ,
.Xr EVP_DigestSignInit 3 ,
.Xr EVP_DigestVerifyInit 3 ,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_CTX_set1_id 3 ,
.Xr EVP_MD_CTX_set_pkey_ctx 3
